          ],
          "content": "Curiosity | Textbook of Science | Grade 6"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.082,
            0.898,
            0.118,
            0.914
          ],
          "content": "152"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.083,
            0.502,
            0.165
          ],
          "content": "Atmospheric Water Generator (AWG) machines collect water from humid air to produce drinkable water. This is done through condensation of water vapour"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.186,
            0.177,
            0.236
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.092,
                0.242,
                0.193,
                0.284
              ],
              "content": "More to know!"
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_9_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.208,
            0.167,
            0.518,
            0.269
          ],
          "content": "by cooling the air. This process is similar to the formation of drops of water outside the glass tumbler filled with ice cold water."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.544,
            0.08,
            0.882,
            0.12
          ],
          "content": "How can you quickly change ice to its liquid state, water?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.549,
            0.12,
            0.883,
            0.238
          ],
          "content": "If we have to change ice into water, and water into water vapour, we have to supply heat to it. If we want to change water into ice, what should be done?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.555,
            0.239,
            0.883,
            0.279
          ],
          "content": "It can be done by placing water in a cold environment,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.478,
            0.279,
            0.882,
            0.299
          ],
          "content": "such as a freezer. Water freezes and is"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.299,
            0.882,
            0.339
          ],
          "content": "converted into ice. If we take the ice out of the freezer, it melts and is converted into water."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.339,
            0.882,
            0.379
          ],
          "content": "Can you think of any other example, besides water, that can change from solid to liquid?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.285,
            0.379,
            0.848,
            0.399
          ],
          "content": "A candle, which is made of wax, is one such example."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.4,
            0.883,
            0.518
          ],
          "content": "How can we turn candle wax into liquid state? How can we change the liquid wax back into solid state? We should cool the liquid wax to change it into a solid. What are the other liquids you have seen which get converted into a solid? Have you ever seen coconut oil getting converted into its solid state during the winter season?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.519,
            0.885,
            0.64
          ],
          "content": "Hence, we can see that water and other substances change their states on heating or cooling. The process of conversion of a solid into liquid state is called **melting**. The process of conversion of liquid into solid state is called **freezing**. Let us check the connection between different states of water through Activity 8.6."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.255,
            0.65,
            0.704,
            0.671
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 8.6: Let us complete the diagram"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.253,
            0.676,
            0.883,
            0.739
          ],
          "content": "Fill up the blank boxes in Fig. 8.5 marked as A, B, C and 1, 2, 3, 4 for conversion of different states of water using the words given in the box. Two words have been filled for you."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
